    "title": "Steven Spielberg Joins the EGOT Club. What Does That Mean?",
    "text": "The Oscars Takeaways Best Picture Winners Unforgettable Looks Stream the Winners Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. Supported by SKIP ADVERTISEMENT Steven Spielberg Joins the EGOT Club. What Does That Mean? Many people were introduced to the idea of an EGOT — winning an Emmy, Grammy, Oscar and Tony — through “30 Rock.” But it’s an actor from the 1980s who deserves the credit. Listen · 9:09 min Share full article Steven Spielberg secured an EGOT for after winning a Grammy for Best Music Film on Sunday. Credit... Richard Shotwell/Invision, via Associated Press By Jennifer Vineyard Feb. 1, 2026 See more of our coverage in your search results. Encuentra más de nuestra cobertura en los resultados de búsqueda. Add The New York Times on Google Agrega The New York Times en Google Follow our live updates for the 2026 Grammy Awards. Steven Spielberg joined the elite club of EGOT winners — those who have collected an Emmy, a Grammy, an Oscar and a Tony — on Sunday when he won the Grammy for best music film for “Music by John Williams .” The 2024 documentary about the film composer was produced by Spielberg, his longtime collaborator. Spielberg first won an Oscar in 1994, after several prior nominations, when “Schindler’s List” earned him trophies for both best picture and best director. He took home his first Primetime Emmy two years later for “A Pinky & the Brain Christmas,” which won for outstanding animated program. He picked up his first Tony in 2022 for his production credit on “A Strange Loop,” which won best musical that year. That same win also made Jennifer Hudson an EGOT winner. Few other directors have ever achieved EGOT status. John Gielgud completed the quartet in 1991 while Mike Nichols did so 20 years later, in 2001. Spielberg is the first new EGOT winner since Justin Paul achieved the milestone in 2024 with an Emmy win. Twenty other people have won EGOTs, including the “Frozen” songwriter Robert Lopez, the only person to do it twice. Elton John completed the feat during the strike-delayed 75th Primetime Emmy Awards, when he won the Emmy for outstanding variety special for “Elton John Live: Farewell From Dodger Stadium.” So who has an EGOT, where did the acronym come from, and what does it really take to earn the accolade? Who is actually in the EGOT club? These are the 22 people who have won EGOTs, along with the year and award that secured the achievement: Richard Rodgers (1962, Emmy) Helen Hayes (1977, Grammy) Rita Moreno (1977, Emmy) John Gielgud (1991, Emmy) Audrey Hepburn (1994, Grammy) Marvin Hamlisch (1995, Emmy) Jonathan Tunick (1997, Tony) Mel Brooks (2001, Tony) Mike Nichols (2001, Emmy) Whoopi Goldberg (2002, Tony) Scott Rudin (2012, Grammy) Robert Lopez (2014, Oscar) John Legend (2018, Emmy) Andrew Lloyd Webber (2018, Emmy) Tim Rice (2018, Emmy) Alan Menken (2020, Emmy) Jennifer Hudson (2022, Tony) Viola Davis (2023, Grammy) Elton John (2024, Emmy) Benj Pasek (2024, Emmy) Justin Paul (2024, Emmy) We are having trouble retrieving the article content. Please enable JavaScript in your browser settings. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
